<!-- ================================================================
     data/model.xml — the model: the whole system as one structure,
     the source of truth every other part is checked against.

     The deck has one territory and many walks through it. This file
     IS the territory: the strata name its layers, the nodes place
     every view in exactly one stratum, and the edges are the roads
     the territory has. The trails are walks in this territory: a
     trail may only open a door the model has a road for, and the
     roads a trail never walks still stand, unclaimed. When the
     logic changes, this file changes first; the rest follows.

     The strata are one descent, each storey one zoom deeper than
     the last: what a person sees; the boundary where the world
     crosses into data and data crosses back; machines together;
     one machine, at work; the words and numbers inside it; the
     machinery of thought, where the founding argument of computing
     is walked — then the fork, the machine seen two ways, drawn as
     two columns that step down together: the tower of abstractions
     on the left above the ways of speaking above the formal
     machines; the machine as built on the right above its
     components above the bedrock. The two columns mirror one
     another, storey for storey, and each is a construction: on
     the right the built-from chain of the machine as it is, on
     the left the built-from chain of the machine as thought —
     the formal machines built up into the ways of speaking,
     the languages built up into the tower. Both sides bottom in
     mathematics itself; nothing is drawn beneath either floor.

     A node is a view by reference: a trail, a promise, or a
     primer. The order inside a stratum is the deck's canonical
     order: trails open the stratum, promises close it, and where
     a stratum ends in the construction the primer grounds it
     last. The trails page walks the strata top to bottom in
     this order.

     An edge is one road, with a kind:
       land  — a landing: where a trail bottoms out. Lit on hover,
               and never drawn climbing: a landing descends or runs
               level, or the strata are wrong.
       cross — a crossing: a door inside a trail's steps. Drawn
               only when it descends exactly one storey; a road
               that climbs, runs level, or skips storeys is walked
               in the views, never drawn, never lit.
       build — built from: the ingredient, the built thing, one
               storey up, never across the fork, never a promise.
     The map draws the strata; the trails page orders itself; the
     hover walks the land and build roads down. All three read this
     one file, and the build checks every walk against it.
     Governed by deck.xsd; the build validates this part alone
     (build.py with the part option) and all parts assembled (the
     default build).
     ================================================================ -->

